Recall that a collection of subsets \(\tau \subset 2^X\) is called a topology on \(X\) if

\(\varnothing , X \in \tau\);
\(\tau\) is closed under finite intersections;
\(\tau\) is closed under arbitrary, even uncountable, unions. The elements of \(\tau\) are called the open subsets of \(X\). A subcollection \(\tau ' \subset \tau\) is called a base for the topology \(\tau\) if every element of \(\tau\) can be represented as a union of some number of elements of \(\tau '\). In \(\mathbb {R}^n\) (more generally, in any metric space), it is customary to take the open balls as a base for the topology, i.e., sets of the form \(\left\{ x \in X \mid \rho (x, x_0) &lt; \varepsilon \right\}\), \(x_0 \in X\), \(\varepsilon &gt; 0\).

