3.2 Definition of a compact operator
Let \(V,W\) be normed spaces, and let \(T: V \to W\) be a linear map. The following statements are equivalent, and different authors may choose any one of them as the primary definition of the map \(T\) being compact:

there exists a neighborhood \(U\) of zero in \(V\) such that \(T(U)\) is a relatively compact subset of \(W\) (i.e., the closure of \(T(U)\) is compact);
there exists a neighborhood \(U\) of zero in \(V\) and a compact subset \(V \subseteq W\) such that \(T(U) \subseteq V\);
there exists a nonempty open set \(U\) in \(V\) such that \(T(U)\) is a relatively compact subset of \(W\).
the image of the unit ball of \(V\) under the operator \(T\) is relatively compact in \(W\);
the image of any bounded subset of \(V\) under the operator \(T\) is relatively compact in \(W\);
for any bounded sequence \((x_n)_{n\in \mathbb {N}}\) in \(V\), the sequence \((Tx_n)_{n\in \mathbb {N}}\) contains a convergent subsequence.
if additionally \(W\) is a Banach space, then these statements are also equivalent to the following: the image of any bounded subset of \(V\) under the operator \(T\) is totally bounded in \(W\).

Denote by \(\mathcal{K}(V,W)\) the set of all compact linear maps. \(\mathcal{K}(V) := \mathcal{K}(V,V)\).

3.2 Normal operators
An operator \(T \in \mathcal{L}(V)\) is called normal if it commutes with its adjoint: \(TT^* = T^*T\). Check for yourself that self-adjoint operators, skew-adjoint operators, and also unitary operators (considered below) are all normal. Hence the properties given in the following theorem apply to all of these operators.







Theorem 7 (Properties of a normal operator) Let \(T \in \mathcal{L}(V)\) be a normal operator. Then

\(\operatorname {Ker} T=\operatorname {Ker} T^*\);
\(\operatorname {Im}T=\operatorname {Im} T^*\);
\(V=\operatorname {Ker} T \oplus \operatorname {Im} T\);
\(T-\lambda I\) is normal for any \(\lambda \in \mathbb {F}\);
If \(\overrightarrow {v} \in V\) and \(\lambda \in \mathbb {F}\), then \(T\overrightarrow {v}=\lambda \overrightarrow {v}\) if and only if \(T^* \overrightarrow {v}=\overline{\lambda } \overrightarrow {v}\);
Eigenvectors of \(T\) corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.

3.1 Order statistics
Let \(\xi_{1}, \ldots , \xi_{n}\) be independent identically distributed variables with CDF \(F\) and density \(p\). Denote by \(\xi_{(1)} \leq \ldots \leq \xi_{(n)}\) their order statistics (the same values, arranged in increasing order). Find:

the density of the vector \(\left(\xi_{(1)}, \ldots , \xi_{(n)}\right)\);
the density of \(\xi_{(k)}\);
the density of the vector \(\left(\xi_{(1)}, \xi_{(n)}\right)\).

